# ABI i386 signals: handler entry and resuming the interrupted code
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Source: the i386 psABI, "Low Level System Information", Function Calling
# Sequence, and the older published revision (abi386-4, chapter 3, the
# paragraph on signals in "Function Calling Sequence"). Neither revision has a
# section on signal delivery. The older one says a returning handler lets the
# process resume with its registers restored and that functions run during
# signal handling have no unusual restrictions; the current one has no such
# sentence. So the resume checks are disputed (INSPECT on FAIL). Reading the
# function-entry rules (stack alignment, direction flag) as applying to a
# handler is an inference, and those checks are disputed as well. The handler
# deliberately changes registers and control words and sets DF before it
# returns, so that restoring them is observable.

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.001
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      disputed
says       A signal handler is entered like any function: ESP plus 4 is a multiple of
           16.
note       Neither revision speaks of signal handlers specifically; this reads the
           function-entry rule (current revision: 16 bytes; the older one: word
           aligned) as covering them. A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/001-entry-16-raise.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.002
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/The Stack Frame (sec-stack-frame)
class      disputed
says       A signal handler that interrupts code whose ESP has any word-aligned value
           (four values mod 16 are tried) is still entered with ESP plus 4 a multiple
           of 16.
note       As signals.001: an inference from the function-entry rule; the current
           revision's 16 bytes, the older one's word alignment. A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/002-entry-16-async.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.003
anchor     i386-psabi:low-level-sys-info#Function Calling Sequence/Registers (table fig-reg-usage)
class      disputed
says       The direction flag is clear on entry to a signal handler even when the
           interrupted code had it set.
note       An inference from the rule that DF is clear at every function entry (both
           revisions). A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/003-entry-df-clear.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.004
anchor     abi386-4 chapter 3, Function Calling Sequence, 'Registers' (paragraph on signals)
class      disputed
says       When a handler returns, the interrupted code resumes with its registers
           restored, so a call to raise() (the handler clobbering EBX, ESI, EDI, EBP,
           DF and the control words) returns with callee-saved registers, DF and both
           control words as they were.
note       Stated by the older revision only. Also an application of the callee-saved
           rules to raise(). A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/004-resume-sync.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.005
anchor     abi386-4 chapter 3, Function Calling Sequence, 'Registers' (paragraph on signals)
class      disputed
says       When a handler returns, the process resumes with its registers (and its
           direction flag) restored: code interrupted with sentinels in EBX, ESI, EDI
           and EBP and DF set finds them again, whatever the handler did with them.
note       Stated by the older revision only. A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/005-resume-async-regs.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.006
anchor     abi386-4 chapter 3, Function Calling Sequence, 'Registers' (paragraph on signals)
class      disputed
says       Code interrupted with a non-default x87 control word finds it unchanged
           after the handler returns, although the handler changed it.
note       Stated by the older revision (registers restored) and consistent with the
           current revision's callee-saved control word. A FAIL is INSPECT.
test       t/006-resume-x87-cw.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

assertion  ABI.i386.signals.007
anchor     abi386-4 chapter 3, Function Calling Sequence, 'Registers' (paragraph on signals)
class      disputed
says       Code interrupted with non-default MXCSR control bits finds them unchanged
           after the handler returns, although the handler changed them.
note       The older revision predates SSE; this follows its registers-restored
           sentence and the current revision's callee-saved MXCSR control bits. A FAIL
           is INSPECT.
test       t/007-resume-mxcsr.c
nomutant   the behaviour belongs to the system's signal delivery, which has no function to plant a fault in; the detectors are validated by abi/control.sh

